# The Manual of Tide Prediction

# Chapter 1\: Gravitational Forces

## The Moon\'s Influence

The gravitational pull of the Moon is the primary driver of ocean tides on Earth\. As the Moon orbits our planet\, its gravitational field creates two tidal bulges in the ocean — one facing the Moon and one on the opposite side of the Earth\.

These bulges are not static\. They follow the Moon\'s orbital path\, creating the familiar rhythm of high and low tides that coastal communities have relied upon for millennia\. Understanding this mechanism is the foundation of all tidal prediction\.

# Chapter 2\: Tidal Patterns

## Spring and Neap Tides

When the Sun\, Moon\, and Earth align during new and full moons\, the combined gravitational forces produce especially large tidal ranges known as spring tides\. Conversely\, when the Sun and Moon are at right angles relative to Earth\, their forces partially cancel\, producing smaller neap tides\.